  "preamble": "Spirantization of the aspirated bilabial stop [pʰ] to the bilabial fricative [ɸ]: the aspiration phase turns into turbulent friction at the lips. It is characteristic of Eastern and Northern Indo-Aryan. In Bengali and Assamese ফ /pʰ/ is commonly realized [ɸ~f]; in eastern Bengali dialects and Sylheti the fricative has replaced the stop outright; and in Nepali non-geminate /pʰ/ is realized [ɸ]. The Greek φ [pʰ] > [f] change is often said to have passed through [ɸ], but there is no direct evidence for that stage, so it belongs under pʰ > f.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Spirantization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Bengali",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ফুল /pʰul/ 'flower'",
          "to": "[ɸul~ful]",
          "note": "Standard Bengali /pʰ/ is realized either as aspirated [pʰ] or, commonly, as the fricative [ɸ~f]; eastern dialects have the fricative throughout."
        },
        {
          "from": "ফল /pʰɔl/ 'fruit'",
          "to": "[ɸɔl]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Assamese",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ফুল /pʰul/ 'flower'",
          "to": "/ɸul/, /ful/",
          "note": "Wiktionary lists the fricative pronunciations alongside the aspirated stop."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Sylheti",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*pʰul 'flower' (cf. Bengali ফুল phul)",
          "to": "ꠙꠥꠟ fúl [ɸul] 'flower'",
          "note": "Sylheti voiceless labials have spirantized to the homorganic fricative (p, pʰ > ɸ), part of its general deaspiration and spirantization."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Nepali",
      "languageFamily": "Northern Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
